WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.669
Film Courage: Please correct me if I’m wrong
but was ALL THE PRESIDENT’S MEN an adaptation?

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:07.850
Woodward and Bernstein’s book?

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:09.320
Dr. Ken Atchity, Producer/Author: Yes.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.180
Film Courage: I just remember in so many scenes
they were running.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.430
Can you talk about the script in any way?

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:22.400
Ken: You know, I’m not as familiar with
the script as I’d like to be because I’d

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.419
like to have recently read the book and the
script.

00:00:25.419 --> 00:00:30.369
One of the things you do in a story like that
is you add the running because there probably

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:32.350
is not a lot of running in real life.

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:37.040
I mean these journalists are probably too
heavy to be running in real life.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.870
Film Courage: They may not look like Robert
Redford either?

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:44.140
Ken: Some of them might but that’s the big
challenge.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.760
Like SPOTLIGHT is an example.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:52.740
They took a lot of, lot of reporting from
Boston Globe and turned it into a dramatic

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:58.460
movie that covered…that collapsed many years
into a few years.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:03.080
You have to take those liberties and that’s
why you end up saying ‘Inspired by a true

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.159
story’ instead of ‘Based on a true story,’
etc.

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:08.420
I’ve been through that many times.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:11.850
But adding action and adding drama is what
you have to do.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:16.880
Film Courage: Right and the scene in SPOTLIGHT
where Stanely Tucci, you don’t see him yet

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.810
but you hear him yelling at his assistant
and I think something is thrown and then the

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.610
look on Mark Ruffalo’s face [who is waiting
to see him] and that right there is enough

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:27.820
to set the stakes.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:28.960
Ken: Right, exactly.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.100
It’s good drama.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:35.889
It involves the audience immediately because
you don’t see what’s happening, so you

00:01:35.889 --> 00:01:39.479
see a reaction to what’s happening so you
have to figure out what’s happening.

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:46.399
And one of the common mistakes that younger
writers make is that they patronize the audience

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:51.280
by explaining too much, by thinking that every
single thing has to be there.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:58.279
And I always say when you’re editing, one
of the top 10 rules of editing are all the

00:01:58.279 --> 00:02:01.549
same…cut…cut…cut…cut…cut.

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:05.749
Because when you’re in doubt about whether
you need something, that alone is a reason

00:02:05.749 --> 00:02:07.340
for cutting it.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.340
You don’t need it.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.580
The audience will make the jump and fill in
the blanks.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.450
And if you don’t let them fill in the blanks
(it’s called painting-by-the-numbers) then

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:20.739
you come across as patronizing to the audience
and they get bored.

00:02:20.739 --> 00:02:21.739
They don’t want to hear every detail.

00:02:21.739 --> 00:02:28.510
You don’t have to say “Your Honor” in
the court scene every single sentence.

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:33.860
You don’t have to say the character’s
name every time you talk to the character.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:39.810
People get this out of their system after
10 years of writing, but at the beginning,

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.810
you don’t know where the lines are.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:43.870
And sense of the audience.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:47.400
One of the things I like to talk about is
the psychology of the audience.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.670
It’s not the psychology of the author that
makes you read a book, you don’t care about

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:52.670
that.

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:57.989
It’s just like your listening to your friend
talking about his latest bout in the hospital.

00:02:57.989 --> 00:03:01.800
I mean how much do you care about how he felt
about every little thing?

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:07.230
You care as long as you can, but if he has
any sense at all he’ll cut it down.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:11.830
And the truth is you don’t care about the
psychology of the characters either.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:16.819
That’s not what’s important, what’s
important is your psychology as always, the

00:03:16.819 --> 00:03:19.129
audience’s psychology.

00:03:19.129 --> 00:03:23.219
And that’s why Hitchcock and Peter Weir
really know what they’re doing.

00:03:23.219 --> 00:03:28.450
That drive away in WITNESS is one of the most
brilliant parts of the film because of the

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:29.800
length of the driveway.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.030
So it was the location choice.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:35.810
He’s telling the location manager I need
a place that has a long, long, long, driveway

00:03:35.810 --> 00:03:41.349
that will give me a 60-second tracking shot.

00:03:41.349 --> 00:03:46.980
And in THE BIRDS when the heroine takes the
flashlight without even testing whether it

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:53.610
works or not and starts heading up the wooden
steps because she hears rustling in the attic,

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:58.879
that is the longest walk up the steps you’ve
ever seen in a film because it is so stupid

00:03:58.879 --> 00:04:03.499
that the audience needs at least three or
four steps to get it out of their system.

00:04:03.499 --> 00:04:08.129
Oh c’mon why does every actress, and why
does she have to have white underwear on,

00:04:08.129 --> 00:04:12.469
and why is it always at the end of a horror
film, and why doesn’t she test it…so once

00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:16.539
you get past that…okay now she’s on the
middle step…and then you go through “Oh,

00:04:16.539 --> 00:04:21.090
my God, why is she…this doesn’t make any
sense…I don’t want to go through this.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:23.669
This doesn’t make any sense.

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:28.421
And then by the time she gets to the top step…you’re
ready, like okay, I paid to get scared, this

00:04:28.421 --> 00:04:29.870
is it.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:36.180
And that is using the psychology of audience,
that’s the timing that the audience needs

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.550
to get into the exact right mood.

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:46.410
It’s just like the speech in JULIUS CESAR
where Marc Anthony comes up after Brutus’s

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:50.229
brilliant speech on the corpse of Julius Cesar.

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:58.990
And Anthony praises Brutus’s speech and
calls him the honorable Brutus and by the

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:04.710
end of his speech he’s turned the entire
crowd against Brutus even though they were

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.470
all cheering for Brutus at the beginning of
his speech.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:15.020
He takes the psychology of the audience and
twists it around in a way that you can see

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:19.900
it coming but you don’t care, you just want
to go there.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:27.210
And nobody cares about Anthony’s psychology
and nobody cares about Brutus’s psychology

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:31.009
(that’s not important) and nobody cares
about Shakespeare’s psychology because nobody

00:05:31.009 --> 00:05:33.490
knows that his psychology is.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:37.270
They said the greatness of Shakespeare and
Homer is that they themselves were nowhere

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:41.010
to be found in their work, their characters
were everywhere.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:45.910
And the characters speak directly to the audience.

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:49.419
That’s the hardest thing about writing is
figuring that out.

